## Owner-directed Application clarification ΓÇö per-user broker/API failure, Web notification, emergency shadow simulation

The Project Owner has now provided the missing product/operational direction. Application owns the authoritative semantics below and no further Owner wait is required for this FCR.

### 1. Scope and isolation

A broker/execution API failure tied to one specific user/principal/account SHALL be contained to that user/account unless evidence proves a broader shared failure.

`USER_A_API_FAILURE != USER_B_FAILURE != APPLICATION_FAILURE != FSATS_FAILURE`

Affected user/account immediately enters a no-new-risk restriction. Other independently healthy users continue normally.

### 2. Failure truth

When the affected user's broker API cannot be contacted at all, Falcon SHALL NOT claim current broker/account/order/position truth.

Falcon preserves:
- last broker-confirmed account snapshot and timestamp;
- last broker-confirmed open positions;
- last broker-confirmed working/protective orders;
- every locally recorded order request/submission attempt and its last proven outcome;
- any ambiguous/unconfirmed submission explicitly as `EXECUTION_TRUTH_UNKNOWN / RECONCILIATION_REQUIRED`.

`LAST_CONFIRMED_STATE != CURRENT_STATE`
`ORDER_SENT != BROKER_ACK != FILL`
`UNKNOWN != FAILED`
`UNKNOWN != SAFE_TO_RETRY`

### 3. Mandatory user-targeted Web alert

Shared Web SHALL receive an Application-authored urgent/action-required notification targeted to the exact affected authenticated principal.

The authoritative notification semantics SHALL include at minimum:
- exact internal user/principal ID and permitted display name;
- affected broker/account/environment reference;
- failure class and first-detected time;
- last successful broker/API contact time;
- `NEW_RISK_RESTRICTED = TRUE`;
- confirmed open positions known before loss of contact;
- confirmed working/protective orders known before loss of contact;
- ambiguous order requests/submissions with exact status `NOT_PROVEN_EXECUTED_OR_NOT_EXECUTED`;
- explicit statement that Falcon currently cannot verify the broker-side state;
- exact required user actions in ordered form;
- correlation/evidence identity and current incident state.

Shared Web owns presentation/copy/layout. It SHALL NOT weaken or reinterpret the Application-authored status/action meaning.

### 4. A-to-Z user actions

The Application shall provide ordered action semantics equivalent to:

A. Do not submit new Falcon trading actions for the affected account while the incident is active.
B. Log in directly to the broker using the broker's independent official interface.
C. Verify the exact current account/balance state.
D. Verify every open position.
E. Verify every working/open/pending order.
F. Verify every stop/protective order that Falcon last knew to exist.
G. For each ambiguous Falcon submission, determine at the broker whether it was rejected, accepted, partially filled, fully filled, cancelled, or remains working.
H. Take any immediately necessary protective action directly at the broker when exposure is unsafe and Falcon has no execution path.
I. Check whether the API key/token/credential is expired, revoked, disabled, permission-reduced, IP-restricted, or otherwise invalid.
J. Repair/renew the API credential as required by the broker.
K. Update the governed Falcon credential reference/secret through the approved secure credential path. Secret bytes SHALL NOT be placed in ordinary Web state, logs, FCRs or notifications.
L. Re-authorize the affected broker/account route only through the governed credential/connection process.
M. Wait for Falcon to re-establish broker connectivity and complete reconciliation before automated trading resumes.

Web may present these as a guided checklist, but completion clicks are user interaction state only and do not themselves prove broker truth.

`USER_CHECKED_ITEM != BROKER_VERIFIED_STATE`

### 5. Emergency FSTSimA shadow case

When API truth is lost, Falcon may open an emergency non-Live FSTSimA shadow case for the affected user/account to assist protection and situational awareness.

This shadow case SHALL NOT become broker/execution truth.

It shall:
- seed from the last broker-confirmed positions/orders and exact confirmation timestamp;
- continue mark-to-market/risk observation using independently available governed market data where available;
- track each confirmed open position as a shadow position;
- track ambiguous submissions as multiple explicit scenarios, at minimum `NOT_EXECUTED`, `PARTIALLY_EXECUTED`, and `FULLY_EXECUTED` where those outcomes are possible;
- show potential exposure/risk under each scenario;
- never collapse scenario output into a claim about actual broker state;
- stop/reconcile the shadow case against authoritative broker truth when connectivity returns.

`SIMULATOR_ESTIMATE != BROKER_TRUTH`
`SHADOW_POSITION != CONFIRMED_LIVE_POSITION`

This uses FSTSimA only as a non-Live protective/diagnostic model. It does not grant Paper/Shadow/Live runtime authority beyond separately governed authorization.

### 6. Owner notification

A separate Owner-facing incident notification SHALL be emitted for a material user broker/API failure. It shall identify:
- internal customer/user ID;
- user display name as authorized for Owner operations;
- affected broker/account reference;
- failure class/time;
- whether current broker truth is unavailable;
- count/list reference of last-confirmed open positions;
- count/list reference of ambiguous submissions;
- whether emergency shadow tracking is active;
- whether the affected user was notified;
- current incident/recovery state.

No API secret/key bytes may appear in the Owner notification.

### 7. Recovery and release

API reconnect alone SHALL NOT remove the restriction.

Required sequence:

`RECONNECT -> AUTHENTICATE -> FETCH ACCOUNT -> FETCH ORDERS -> FETCH FILLS -> FETCH POSITIONS -> FETCH PROTECTION STATE -> RECONCILE AGAINST LAST CONFIRMED + AMBIGUOUS REQUESTS + SHADOW CASE -> RESOLVE ALL MATERIAL DIFFERENCES -> SAFETY/RISK CHECK -> RELEASE USER-SCOPED RESTRICTION`

If any material execution truth remains unknown, the affected user/account remains restricted.

### 8. Web/Application ownership boundary

Application owns:
- incident targeting and user/account scope;
- failure/current-truth semantics;
- urgency/action-required classification;
- authoritative ordered action requirements;
- confirmed vs ambiguous position/order meaning;
- shadow-simulation semantic limits;
- recovery/release conditions;
- Owner incident semantics.

Shared Web owns:
- visual presentation;
- checklist interaction;
- notification center/popup/mobile presentation;
- human-readable rendering;
- acknowledgement UX.

Mandatory distinctions:

`TARGETED_TO_USER != AUTHORIZED_BY_USER`
`URGENT != ACTION_REQUIRED`
`UI_CLICK != BUSINESS_AUTHORIZATION`
`REQUEST_SENT != REQUEST_ACCEPTED != ACTION_COMPLETED`
`NOTIFICATION_DELIVERED != INCIDENT_RESOLVED`
`SIMULATOR_ESTIMATE != BROKER_TRUTH`

### 9. Application disposition

`APPLICATION_SEMANTICS = DEFINED_BY_OWNER_DIRECTION`
`APPLICATION_NEXT_ACTION = INCORPORATE_IN_GOVERNED_FSATS_REMEDIATION/AMENDMENT_AND_TESTS`
`WEB_NEXT_ACTION = VERIFY_COMPATIBILITY_AND MATERIALIZE WEB-OWNED UX AGAINST THESE SEMANTICS`

HANDOFF_UPDATE_REQUIRED: Waiting On=WEB; Next Required Action=Shared Web shall consume this Owner-directed Application semantic definition, verify compatibility, and materialize the Web-owned user/Owner incident notification and guided-action UX without inventing broker truth or Application authority.; Target Foundation Stage/WP=N/A ΓÇö APPLICATION/WEB CROSS-WORKSTREAM SEMANTIC BINDING; Review Trigger=Before affected Guardian/user-incident notification UX freeze and after any material Application semantic change.

## Owner clarification ΓÇö Owner outreach/escalation role when affected user is unreachable

The Project Owner clarified the human-escalation boundary for user-scoped broker/API incidents.

### 1. Authority boundary

Only the affected user/account holder may authorize or perform actions on that user's broker account/portfolio unless an external broker/legal mechanism explicitly provides otherwise.

Therefore:
- Falcon does **not** gain authority to trade or modify the user's broker account merely because the API failed;
- the Project Owner does **not** gain authority to trade or modify the user's broker account;
- Shared Web does **not** gain such authority;
- no other Falcon actor may substitute itself for the user;
- broker-side action still depends on the user's own authenticated broker access/authorization under the broker's rules.

Mandatory distinction:

`OWNER_ESCALATION_ROLE != PORTFOLIO_EXECUTION_AUTHORITY`

### 2. Simultaneous Owner notification

At the same time the user-scoped incident begins and the affected user is notified, the Project Owner SHALL also receive an Owner-facing incident notification containing the governed incident summary needed for outreach and oversight, including as applicable:
- internal user/customer ID;
- permitted display name;
- affected broker/account reference;
- incident start time/state;
- last confirmed execution/account state time;
- known/unknown exposure summary;
- ambiguous order count/state;
- whether the user has acknowledged/responded;
- current guided-recovery step;
- current market-risk priority/escalation state;
- no API key/secret/token bytes.

### 3. Owner human-outreach responsibility

If the affected user does not respond promptly through Web, the Owner becomes the human outreach/escalation coordinator, using the contact channels Falcon/operations legitimately has for that customer, such as phone, email, messaging, or other approved contact methods.

The purpose is to get the user back into the recovery loop, not to act in the user's place.

The Owner may guide the user verbally using the same Falcon/AI recovery instructions exposed through the Web incident flow. The Owner may tell the user which broker screens to inspect, what screenshot/evidence to provide, how to verify positions/orders/fills/protection, and where to update broker API credentials in the governed Web flow.

### 4. Guidance provenance

If the Owner relays Falcon guidance to the user outside the Web channel, the incident record should preserve that an Owner-mediated contact occurred, the time/channel used, whether the user was reached, and the recovery step/result reported back.

Owner-mediated guidance remains guidance only:

`OWNER_RELAYED_GUIDANCE != USER_ACTION`
`OWNER_CONTACTED_USER != INCIDENT_RESOLVED`
`USER_REPORT != BROKER_CONFIRMED_STATE`

### 5. No-response handling

If the user remains unreachable:
- keep the affected user/account under no-new-risk restriction;
- continue market/provider monitoring and non-Live scenario/risk tracking;
- keep exposure uncertainty explicit;
- continue Owner outreach/escalation attempts according to the incident policy;
- do not fabricate a broker-side recovery or portfolio action that Falcon/Owner cannot authorize.

This clarification supplements the existing FCR-0095 semantics. Shared Web should expose an Owner-facing incident panel/status suitable for this outreach role without converting the Owner into broker execution authority.

## Owner correction ΓÇö strict Application/Web responsibility boundary

The Project Owner corrected the prior Application-side framing to prevent Trading/FSATS from absorbing Web/customer-contact responsibilities.

### Application / FSATS responsibility
Application defines only the trading/business incident semantics and the exact information/action request required to protect the affected trading state. This includes, as applicable:
- incident classification and affected trading scope;
- what is known, unknown, stale, user-reported, screenshot-observed or broker-confirmed;
- which position/order/execution uncertainty has higher trading-risk priority;
- whether new risk must remain frozen;
- the exact question, confirmation or broker-side fact needed next from the affected user;
- the safe trading/reconciliation action sequence once that fact is returned;
- credential-recovery requirement at the semantic level (for example: broker credentials must be renewed/replaced and revalidated) without owning the credential-entry UX;
- recovery/release criteria after broker connectivity returns;
- the response outcome that Application needs back from Web to continue the trading recovery state machine.

### Shared Web responsibility
Shared Web owns all presentation and interaction mechanics, including:
- how requests are shown to the user or Owner;
- how notifications, prompts, chat, forms, uploads, screenshots and acknowledgements are implemented;
- how the Owner and user interact through the Web experience;
- how contact/communication workflows are presented or coordinated;
- any Web-owned handling of customer/profile/contact information;
- how API/secret update fields or credential-management UI are rendered, subject to governed credential/security boundaries.

Application SHALL NOT define or own customer contact data, phone/email/SMS workflow, Web dashboard layout, notification-delivery mechanics, contact-attempt tracking, or user-profile/contact-information management.

### Required cross-workstream contract

The boundary is:

`APPLICATION REQUESTS REQUIRED BUSINESS/TRADING FACT OR ACTION`
`-> WEB COMMUNICATES / COLLECTS THROUGH ITS OWN UX`
`-> WEB RETURNS THE REQUESTED RESULT / EVIDENCE`
`-> APPLICATION CONTINUES TRADING INCIDENT DECISION / RECONCILIATION`

Mandatory distinctions:

`APPLICATION_REQUIRED_INFORMATION != CUSTOMER_CONTACT_DATA`
`APPLICATION_ACTION_SEMANTICS != WEB_INTERACTION_DESIGN`
`APPLICATION_REQUEST != WEB_NOTIFICATION_IMPLEMENTATION`
`WEB_RESPONSE_TRANSPORT != TRADING_DECISION_AUTHORITY`

This correction supersedes any prior Application-side wording that prescribed Owner dashboard fields such as phone/SMS/email/contact-attempt tracking or otherwise crossed into Web/customer-contact ownership. It does not change the trading safety semantics already supplied for per-user broker/API failure.

FCR-0095 remains `Waiting On: WEB`; Shared Web shall consume this corrected boundary when materializing the UX and return only the governed response/evidence Application requires.

